unicate much more with my  eyes and my eyebrow  when I was when I am very math so it  depends a little bit on how long this  will continue if we have to wear the  mass in public for the next couple of  months maybe then we clearly learn these  new techniques yes is it fair to say  that face masks represent just one  aspect of a larger problem during this  pandemic and it is social isolation yes  that's right face masks are just a  visible sign of it but I think the  social isolation phenomena are much more  severe and a much wider or broader than  the face mask alone so people who do not  live in families who do not live in in  larger communities have learned to to  not seeing their colleagues their  friends their relatives for two or three  months and this is a huge problem  you've mentioned also in the past  physical contact basic physical contact  something that we try and avoid with  many people right now that's also a form  of isolation to not have that correct  that's right and I mean it's a cultural  issue I was visiting China a couple of  years ago and and right afterwards was  in Japan so Chinese keep context much  more than Japanese for granted ooh  German are probably somewhere in the  middle and and so it's a difficult thing  if you are not used to that situation  not being able to touch each other  it feels awkward and and we humans also  learned in our years of many million  years of evolution to to to console each  other by physical touch and contact what  kind of a psychological burden is that  put on people to have to forgo a lot of  these social interactions I think it  puts a huge burden on our studies  starting in the 1950s that have shown  that children who were hospitalized for  a few months or longer because of severe  illnesses had severe impairments of  their  cognitive but also the social  development we are working together on a  corona project with people from 10  different countries and just now the  data from Kazakhstan came in in  Kazakhstan between mid March and late  late May more than 800 people have  killed themselves compared to only 14 in  the year before so that signal said  something is going on we don't know yet  whether it also is because some people  have financial worries or other  stressors but I think social isolation  is a big factor contributing to that all  right an easily overlooked aspect of the  pandemic professor Ralph and dick from  go to University and Frankfurt thank you  for joining us and time now to answer  your questions about kovat 19 over to  our science correspondent derrick  williams how will those who sustained  long-term damage from Kovach 19 respond  to the flu this fall a lot of experts  are worried that this winter we're going  to see this kind of perfect respiratory  disease storm that a feared second wave  of kovat 19 will will coincide with the  yearly flu season now if that happens it  could easily easily bring health systems  really to the brink when it comes to  influenza one of the one of the most  vulnerable populations in the coming  fall of winter will unquestionably be  those people who have already been been  ravaged by kovat 19 for them catching  the flu in the months ahead could  quickly turn life-threatening that's one  reason why doctors are encouraging as  many people as possible to get a flu  shot this year most especially those who  suffered badly from Kovan 19 and might  have sustained lung damage from it a flu  shot won't provide perfect protection  but it's it's it's much much better than  nothing  contests differentiate between Tsar's  Cove - and the corona viruses that cause  colds this is a little complicated so  stick with me when it comes to testing  you have to distinguish between  sensitivity and specificity now  sensitivity describes how likely a test  is to to properly detect an infection  with the virus so so it affects what are  called false negative results some  specificity on the other hand describes  how likely or unlikely 